#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:5
msgid "Kernel Page Tables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:8
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:10
msgid ""
"The kernel page tables are created early during boot and, unlike the page "
"tables of user processes, most of them remain static throughout the system "
"lifetime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:14
msgid ""
"Every architecture has a direct map (also called linear map) that maps the "
"physical memory at a fixed offset, so that a physical address can be "
"translated to a kernel virtual address with simple arithmetic. On most "
"architectures the direct map is a part of the kernel page tables, with a few "
"exceptions described in the `Direct map`_ section below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:20
msgid ""
"On 32-bit systems with high memory the direct map covers only a part of the "
"physical memory, see Documentation/mm/highmem.rst."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:23
msgid ""
"The vmalloc area, present on every architecture with an MMU, is used for "
"allocations of virtually contiguous memory whose backing pages are not "
"necessarily physically contiguous, and for mapping of the device memory. Its "
"page tables are created and torn down at runtime, see Documentation/mm/"
"vmalloc.rst."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:29
msgid ""
"Architectures that use the `SPARSEMEM_VMEMMAP` memory model reserve a range "
"of kernel address space for the memory map, so that `struct page` objects "
"appear as a virtually contiguous array indexed by the page frame number, see "
"Documentation/mm/memory-model.rst."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:34
msgid ""
"Besides these, the kernel image may be mapped in a dedicated part of the "
"kernel address space rather than accessed through the direct map. In that "
"case its mapping is an alias of the direct map of the physical memory the "
"image occupies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:39
msgid ""
"The rest of the kernel address space is architecture specific. For instance, "
"x86 has a region for the EFI runtime services and s390 has a region for the "
"code that has to run in the 31-bit addressing mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:44
msgid "Direct map"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:46
msgid ""
"On most architectures the direct map is an ordinary part of the kernel page "
"tables. It is created early during boot with the largest pages the hardware "
"and the kernel configuration allow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:50
msgid "Several architectures are different."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:53
msgid "MIPS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:55
msgid ""
"MIPS does not map the physical memory with page tables at all. Instead, a "
"part of the kernel virtual address space is a window into the physical "
"address space: the hardware translates the addresses that fall into that "
"window by a fixed transformation of the address bits, without walking the "
"page tables and without using the TLB. The memory attributes, such as "
"cacheability and the privilege level required to access the memory, are a "
"property of the window rather than of an individual page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:63
msgid ""
"There are no page table entries describing the direct map, so its properties "
"cannot be changed for an individual page. On 32-bit systems the window "
"covers only 512 MiB of the physical memory, so everything above that is high "
"memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:68 ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:332
msgid "LoongArch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:70
msgid ""
"Like MIPS, LoongArch maps the physical memory with a hardware window. The "
"window covers 256 TiB of the physical address space on 64-bit and 512 MiB on "
"32-bit systems, and everything above that is high memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:74
msgid ""
"The window occupies the lower part of the kernel address space. The upper "
"part, which includes the vmalloc area, is mapped with kernel page tables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:78
msgid "PowerPC with the hash MMU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:80
msgid ""
"On 64-bit PowerPC systems with the hash MMU the direct map does not exist in "
"the Linux page tables. It is installed into the hardware hash page table "
"early during boot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:84
msgid ""
"Modifying such mappings requires updating the hash page table directly, and "
"the hash MMU code implements this only for the kernel image permissions, "
"`debug_pagealloc` and KFENCE."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:88
msgid ""
"With the radix MMU the direct map is a part of the ordinary kernel page "
"tables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:92
msgid "Modifying the kernel page tables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:94
msgid ""
"Except for the vmalloc area, the kernel page tables are mostly static. "
"Still, there are cases when the permissions of existing kernel mappings have "
"to be updated, for instance when a module is loaded and its text becomes "
"read-only and executable, or when a page is temporarily removed from the "
"direct map to reduce its exposure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:100
msgid ""
"There are two families of functions for this, both declared in `include/"
"linux/set_memory.h`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:103
msgid ""
"`set_memory_*()` change permissions of an arbitrary kernel mapping. They "
"take a kernel virtual address and the number of pages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:106
msgid ""
"`set_direct_map_*()` change permissions of the direct mapping for the range "
"of page frames starting at the page represented by a `struct page`. They "
"take a `struct page` pointer and the number of pages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:110
msgid ""
"Architectures that implement `set_memory()` select "
"`CONFIG_ARCH_HAS_SET_MEMORY`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:112
msgid ""
"Architectures that implement `set_direct_map()` select "
"`CONFIG_ARCH_HAS_SET_DIRECT_MAP`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:116
msgid "Common semantics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:119
msgid "Ranges"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:121
msgid ""
"The `set_memory()` functions expect a range described by a start address and "
"a number of pages. The address must be page aligned and the entire range "
"must be covered by page table entries the architecture knows how to update."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:125
msgid ""
"The entries may be marked as not present, set_memory_p() and "
"set_memory_valid() exist exactly to bring such a mapping back."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:128
msgid ""
"When a range does not qualify, an architecture will usually say so by "
"returning an error and sometimes by a WARN()ing as well, unless it prefers "
"to keep it to itself and return success, see `Architecture specific "
"differences`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:132
msgid ""
"The `set_direct_map()` functions expect a range described by the first "
"`struct page` and a number of pages, and they update the direct map starting "
"at that page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:136
msgid ""
"The pages that follow the first one are updated regardless of what they are, "
"so the caller has to make sure that the range does not extend beyond the "
"memory it owns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:140
msgid ""
"Some architectures cannot split a large mapping, and they reject a range "
"that is a part of one, see `Architecture specific differences`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:143
msgid ""
"Calling `set_memory()` with the number of pages set to zero is a no-op that "
"returns success, except on arm64, where doing nothing to the wrong address "
"is still an error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:148
msgid "Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:150
msgid ""
"A physical page may be mapped several times, for instance in the direct map "
"and in the vmalloc area, and the permissions of these mappings may differ."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:153
msgid ""
"Whether the direct map alias is updated by a `set_memory()` call, and which "
"permission bits make it there, is entirely up to the architecture, and there "
"is not much agreement between them, see `Architecture specific differences`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:158
msgid ""
"Relying on that is a gamble; code that needs the direct map alias to change "
"should say so with the `set_direct_map()` APIs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:162
msgid "Failures and partial updates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:164
msgid ""
"Both families of functions return 0 on success and a negative error code on "
"failure. The most common failures are `-EINVAL` for a range that cannot be "
"handled and `-ENOMEM` when splitting a large mapping fails to allocate a "
"page table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:169
msgid ""
"Some of the range checks happen upfront, so their failure leaves the page "
"tables unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:172
msgid "**The update is not atomic and there is no rollback.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:174
msgid ""
"The architecture implementations walk the range and update the page tables "
"as they go, and they stop at the first entry that cannot be updated. When an "
"error is returned, an arbitrary prefix of the range may have been updated "
"already, and the same is true for the direct map alias when the architecture "
"updates it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:179
msgid ""
"For example, when a range spans two large mappings and splitting the second "
"one fails because there is no memory for a page table, the first one is "
"already split and updated by the time the error is returned."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:183
msgid ""
"None of the APIs inform the caller where in the range they failed, so "
"reverting such a partial update is possible in principle but unreliable in "
"practice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:186
msgid ""
"The caller may try to restore the original permissions over the entire "
"range, but that revert goes through the very code that has just failed, "
"which does not inspire much confidence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:190
msgid ""
"The callers should therefore be prepared to give up on the memory in "
"question: leak it or panic, but never return it to the allocator before the "
"permissions are restored and never assume that the requested permissions are "
"in effect. Neither option is appealing, but both beat handing out a page "
"whose permissions nobody knows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:197
msgid "TLB flushing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:199
msgid ""
"The `set_memory()` functions flush the TLB for the affected range before "
"they return, so that the new permissions are in effect for every CPU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:202
msgid ""
"The `set_direct_map()` functions have `_noflush` in their names because when "
"they were first introduced on x86, the intention was that the TLB flushing "
"could be optimized by letting the caller handle it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:206
msgid ""
"For example, vfree() batches the TLB flushes for the areas allocated with "
"`VM_FLUSH_RESET_PERMS`, folding the flush of the direct map into the flush "
"it has to do for the vmalloc mapping anyway."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:210
msgid ""
"Some architectures flush the TLB in the `_noflush` functions anyway, so the "
"name is best read as a suggestion. It does not make the flush by the caller "
"unnecessary, it only makes it more expensive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:214
msgid ""
"A caller that changes the permissions to more restrictive ones must flush "
"the TLB itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:218
msgid "Context"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:220
msgid ""
"Architectures use different locking mechanisms to synchronize kernel page "
"table updates, and both families of functions may sleep, for instance when "
"they allocate memory to split a large mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:224
msgid ""
"The caller cannot presume it is safe to call these APIs from an atomic "
"context."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:226
msgid ""
"The `set_direct_map()` functions must not be called for high memory pages, "
"which have no direct map alias to update."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:230
msgid "Unimplemented APIs silently succeed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:232
msgid ""
"When an architecture does not implement these APIs, the generic stubs in "
"`include/linux/set_memory.h` return 0, that is, they report success for work "
"they have no intention of doing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:236
msgid ""
"The same happens inside several architecture implementations. The arm64 "
"`set_direct_map()` functions return 0 when can_set_direct_map() is false, "
"and the LoongArch `set_memory()` functions return 0 for the addresses in its "
"windowed direct mapping, which is not backed by page tables at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:241
msgid ""
"Returning success without actually updating the page tables is a deliberate "
"trade-off that keeps the callers free of `#ifdef`\\ s, but they have to "
"realize:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:244
msgid ""
"**a return value of 0 does not imply that the permissions were actually "
"changed.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:247
msgid ""
"For best-effort hardening that is good enough. When correctness or security "
"depends on the permissions, the caller has to make sure the architecture "
"really implements what it needs. For instance, secretmem depends on "
"`CONFIG_ARCH_HAS_SET_DIRECT_MAP` and calls can_set_direct_map() at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:253
msgid "Architecture specific differences"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:255
msgid ""
"The APIs are implemented by seven architectures and, beyond the common "
"semantics described above, their behaviour differs in several respects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:258
msgid "Which of the APIs are implemented:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:261
msgid "Arch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:261
msgid "`ARCH_HAS_SET_MEMORY`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:261
msgid "`ARCH_HAS_SET_DIRECT_MAP`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:263
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:293
msgid "arm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:263
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:264
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:265
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:266
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:268
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:269
msgid "yes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:263
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:266
msgid "no"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:264
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:303
msgid "arm64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:265
msgid "loongarch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:266
msgid "powerpc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:267
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:354
msgid "riscv"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:267
msgid "yes (MMU only)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:268
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:369
msgid "s390"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:269
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:387
msgid "x86"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:272
msgid ""
"Only set_memory_ro(), set_memory_rw(), set_memory_x() and set_memory_nx() "
"are available everywhere, and even these are not universal: some "
"architectures restrict the ranges that can be modified, for instance arm64 "
"rejects direct map addresses and accepts only addresses in vmalloc space. "
"The architectures that may run on hardware without an execute permission "
"bit, like x86 and s390, silently skip the update of the executable bit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:279
msgid ""
"set_memory_rox() has a generic implementation that calls set_memory_ro() and "
"set_memory_x() in turn; PowerPC, s390 and x86 override it with a single-pass "
"version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:283
msgid ""
"Several architectures define additional APIs. Some of those may have "
"identical semantics but different names. For example, making a mapping "
"present or not present is spelled differently: set_memory_p() and "
"set_memory_np() on x86 and PowerPC, set_memory_valid() on arm and arm64."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:288
msgid ""
"The direct map and the kernel image are normally mapped with the largest "
"possible pages, and changing the permissions of a single page inside such a "
"mapping requires splitting it, which not every architecture can do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:295
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:344
msgid "Does not implement `set_direct_map()`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:296
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:305
msgid "Provides set_memory_valid()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:297
msgid ""
"set_memory_ro(), set_memory_rw(), set_memory_x() and set_memory_nx() accept "
"only vmalloc and module addresses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:299
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:314
msgid "set_memory_valid() accepts any address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:300
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:336
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:351
msgid "Does not update mapping aliases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:306
msgid ""
"Provides the memory encryption helpers, which are effective only when the "
"kernel runs as a confidential guest."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:308
msgid ""
"set_memory_ro(), set_memory_rw(), set_memory_x() and set_memory_nx() accept "
"only vmalloc and module addresses:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:311
msgid "the range must fit in the VM area that contains its start"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:312
msgid "the VM area must have `VM_ALLOC` set and `VM_ALLOW_HUGE_VMAP` clear"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:315
msgid "The encryption helpers accept only direct map addresses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:316
msgid ""
"The `set_memory()` functions propagate the read-only and the read-write "
"changes to the direct map alias when `rodata=full` is in effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:318
msgid ""
"Splits leaf mappings before the update on the hardware that supports it. The "
"split itself may be partial when it fails midway, but the permissions are "
"left untouched in that case. Without support for splitting large mappings, a "
"range that covers a leaf entry only partially fails with a WARN()ing and `-"
"EINVAL`. If a range spans one or more full leaf entries and a partial leaf "
"entry, the permissions of the full leaf entries are updated before the "
"failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:325
msgid ""
"The `set_direct_map()` functions return 0 without doing anything when the "
"direct map cannot be modified, see can_set_direct_map()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:327
msgid ""
"Skips the TLB flush in `set_memory()` when the update only turns an invalid "
"mapping into a valid one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:329
msgid "Does not flush TLB in `set_direct_map()`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:334
msgid ""
"Accepts only the addresses above the hardware window and silently returns "
"success for the rest, see `Direct map`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:337
msgid ""
"Does not split anything: a leaf entry is updated as a whole, which changes "
"the permissions of the entire large mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:339
#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:366
msgid "Flushes the TLB in `set_direct_map()`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:342
msgid "PowerPC"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:345
msgid "Provides set_memory_np() and set_memory_p()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:346
msgid "Rejects huge vmalloc mappings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:347
msgid ""
"With the hash MMU on 64-bit systems accepts nothing but the vmalloc and the "
"I/O regions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:349
msgid ""
"With the radix MMU accepts direct map addresses, but still cannot split a "
"large mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:356
msgid "Implements both APIs only when the MMU is enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:357
msgid "Provides set_memory_rw_nx()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:358
msgid ""
"The `set_memory()` functions accept any mapped kernel address, including the "
"direct map, but a vmalloc range must have the `pages` array of its VM area "
"populated, which rules out vmap() and ioremap() mappings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:361
msgid ""
"On 64-bit systems the `set_memory()` functions update the direct map alias "
"of a vmalloc range, including the executable bit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:363
msgid ""
"Does not split vmalloc ranges: a leaf entry is updated as a whole, which "
"changes the permissions of the entire large mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:365
msgid "Splits the direct map on 64-bit systems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:371
msgid ""
"Provides set_memory_4k(), set_memory_rwnx() and the `__set_memory_*(start, "
"end)` variants that take a range rather than a page count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:374
msgid ""
"The `set_memory()` functions accept any mapped kernel address, including the "
"direct map."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:376
msgid ""
"Skips the update of the executable bit when the hardware has no support for "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:378
msgid ""
"The `set_memory()` functions propagate only the read-only and read-write "
"changes to the direct map alias of a `VM_ALLOC` area, and deliberately not "
"the executable bit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:381
msgid ""
"Splits leaf PUD and PMD entries when the range is not aligned to them or "
"when set_memory_4k() is requested."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:383
msgid ""
"Updates the page table entries with instructions that invalidate the "
"corresponding TLB entries, so no separate flush is needed anywhere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:389
msgid "Provides the largest set of operations on top of the common ones:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:391
msgid ""
"the cache attribute helpers: set_memory_uc(), set_memory_wc(), "
"set_memory_wb()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:393
msgid "presence control: set_memory_np() and set_memory_p()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:394
msgid "set_memory_4k()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:395
msgid "set_memory_global() and set_memory_nonglobal()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:396
msgid ""
"the array variants that operate on `struct page` arrays or arrays of virtual "
"addresses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:398
msgid "memory encryption: set_memory_encrypted() and set_memory_decrypted()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:400
msgid ""
"The `set_memory()` functions accept any mapped kernel address, including the "
"direct map, and silently succeed for the unmapped holes inside it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:402
msgid ""
"Does nothing in set_memory_x() and set_memory_nx() when the CPU has no "
"execute permission bit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:404
msgid ""
"The `set_memory()` functions apply the change to the direct map alias and, "
"for the kernel image, to the high kernel mapping. The NX bit is never "
"propagated, so that the direct map stays non-executable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:407
msgid ""
"Splits large mappings on demand and can collapse them back when the "
"permissions become uniform again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../mm/kernel-page-tables.rst:409
msgid ""
"Does not flush the TLB in `set_direct_map()`, but splitting a large mapping "
"flushes it anyway."
msgstr ""
